(>> Solution to Question 3:) Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F1 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: Explain the Ternary Conditional Operator \(x if condition else y\). Compare its syntax, readability, evaluation semantics, and nesting with traditional if-else blocks.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   1. Motivation & Syntax of Python's Conditional Expression:) Tj
T*
(   Codified in PEP 308, Python supports conditional expressions \(commonly known as the ternary) Tj
T*
(   operator\):) Tj
T*
(   Syntax: <expression_if_true> if <condition> else <expression_if_false>) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   2. Key Semantic Properties:) Tj
T*
(   1. It is an expression, not a statement. It evaluates to a concrete value and can thus be used) Tj
T*
(   on the right-hand side of assignments, inside return statements, within lambda functions, or as) Tj
T*
(   arguments to other functions.) Tj
T*
(   2. Lazy Evaluation / Short-Circuiting: Only one of the two sub-expressions is evaluated. If) Tj
T*
(   <condition> is truthy, <expression_if_true> is evaluated and <expression_if_false> is) Tj
T*
(   completely ignored.) Tj
T*
(   3. Mandatory else: Unlike traditional if statements where else is optional, ternary expressions) Tj
T*
(   strictly require an else clause.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   3. Python Code Demonstration:) Tj
T*
(   # 1. Assignment using ternary expression) Tj
T*
(   age = 20) Tj
T*
(   status = "Eligible to Vote" if age >= 18 else "Minor") Tj
T*
(   print\(f"Status: {status}"\)) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   # 2. Nested Ternary Expression \(Multi-State Classification\)) Tj
T*
(   def classify_number\(n: int\) -> str:) Tj
T*
(   # Syntax: Positive if n > 0 else \(Zero if n == 0 else Negative\)) Tj
T*
(   return "Positive" if n > 0 else "Zero" if n == 0 else "Negative") Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   print\(f"10 -> {classify_number\(10\)} | 0 -> {classify_number\(0\)} | -5 -> {classify_number\(-5\)}"\)) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
ET

endstream
